course: chemistry
lesson: 10
title: Cooking is chemistry
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every kitchen is a chemistry lab: heat, acid and water change food into new substances with new tastes, smells and textures.
explain: Fry a piece of meat and the outside turns brown and smells delicious, because sugars and proteins react and make hundreds of new flavour molecules.
explain: Some changes are about water: popcorn bursts when water inside turns to steam, and rice swells as it soaks water into its starch.
explain: Others are about proteins: boiling sets an egg, and even lemon juice can change the surface of meat or fish without any heat.
explain: Knowing the chemistry also keeps you safe: hot caramel, metal in the microwave and old rice all have rules for good reasons.
fact: Egg white sets at a lower temperature than the yolk, so a 4-minute egg has a runny yolk and a 10-minute egg a hard one.
fact: An over-boiled egg can get a greenish ring around the yolk, where iron from the yolk joins sulphur from the white.
fact: Browning of meat, toast and fried food is the Maillard reaction between sugars and proteins at high heat; boiled meat does not brown because water stays at about 100 °C.
fact: Caramel is sugar broken down by strong heat into new substances; hot caramel is much hotter than boiling water and sticks to skin.
fact: Popcorn pops because water inside each kernel turns to steam until the hard shell bursts; dried-out kernels pop badly.
fact: Jelly sets because gelatine molecules form a loose net that traps water; fresh pineapple contains a substance that cuts gelatine and stops it setting.
fact: Baking powder contains a dry acid and baking soda that react when wet and warm, making carbon dioxide bubbles; too much makes food taste soapy and bitter.
fact: Slow cooking breaks down the sharp chemicals in onions and lets their sugars brown, so they turn soft and sweet.
fact: The chemicals that make you cry are made only when an onion is cut.
fact: Acid such as lemon or lime juice unfolds proteins as heat does; fish soaked in lime juice turns white but has not been heated.
fact: Microwaves make water molecules wiggle very fast, which heats the food, often unevenly, so stirring halfway mixes hot and cooler parts; metal and foil must never go in a microwave because they can spark.
fact: Rice softens and swells as it soaks up water into its starch; leftover rice should be cooled quickly and kept in the fridge, because some germs grow in it fast at room temperature.
exercise: Why does a 4-minute egg have a runny yolk but a 10-minute egg a hard one? | The yolk sets more slowly | The shell lets heat in only after 5 minutes | The yolk is full of tiny air bubbles | The white melts in long boiling
exercise: Why does meat turn brown in a hot frying pan? | Sugars and proteins react and make new flavour molecules | The hot pan paints a thin brown layer of metal onto the meat as it cooks | The meat gets dirty from the oil | The meat rusts in the heat
exercise: What happens when sugar turns into golden caramel? | It breaks down into new substances | It only melts and is still plain white sugar, just runny and hot | It turns into crunchy grains of salt | It soaks up air from the room and browns
exercise: Why does popcorn pop? | Its water turns to steam | Air from outside is sucked inside the kernel until it bursts | The oil explodes in the pan | The kernel is full of gunpowder
exercise: Why does jelly set in the fridge? | Gelatine molecules make a net that traps the water | The water in the jelly freezes into ice crystals in the cold | The sugar turns into a solid | The fridge pushes the jelly together
exercise: What makes pancakes with baking powder go fluffy? | Carbon dioxide bubbles | Air whisked into the batter by the hot frying pan as it cooks | Steam from the eggs alone | Butter rising to the top
exercise: Why do onions turn sweet when cooked slowly? | Heat breaks down the sharp chemicals and browns the sugars | Cooks always add spoonfuls of sugar to them in secret | The onion turns into a fruit | Slow heat makes them grow sugar
exercise: What does lemon juice do to meat in a marinade? | Its acid unfolds proteins on the surface, a bit like cooking | It dissolves the meat completely, until only the bones and juice are left | It freezes the meat | It makes the meat sweet
exercise: How does a microwave heat soup? | It makes water molecules wiggle very fast | It blows very hot air all round the soup | It heats the bowl first, then the soup | It shines a hot light on the soup
exercise: Why does rice get soft and bigger in boiling water? | Its starch soaks up water | The rice grains hatch open in the hot water | Air bubbles fill the grains | The salt in the pan makes it grow
exercise: Why is there sometimes a green ring around a hard-boiled yolk? | Iron from the yolk joined sulphur from the white | The egg was old and had gone mouldy | The hen ate lots of green grass | The pan leaked metal into it
exercise: Why does boiled chicken not go brown? | Water cannot get hotter than about 100 °C, too cool for browning | Chicken has no protein in it at all, so nothing can turn brown | Boiling washes the brown off | Brown colour dissolves in water
exercise: Why must metal never go in a microwave? | It can spark | It melts into the food | It makes the food cold | It makes the microwave too quiet
exercise: Why should leftover rice go in the fridge quickly? | Some germs grow fast in it at room temperature | It slowly turns back into hard raw rice if it is left out | It goes hard as stone | It loses all its flavour at once
test: Your cousin says fish soaked in lime juice is "cooked, same as frying". What is the truth? | It turns white as proteins unfold, but it has not been heated | It is fully cooked and boiling hot, just like fish from a frying pan | Lime juice freezes the fish | Nothing happens to the fish at all
test: "why's the jelly packet say no fresh pineapple, that's mad" Best answer? | Fresh pineapple has a substance that cuts up gelatine | Pineapple makes the jelly far too sweet to eat | Pineapple turns jelly into juice gas | Pineapple is poisonous with sugar
test: What would happen if you used a handful of baking powder instead of a teaspoon? | The pancakes would taste soapy and bitter | They would be perfect, only twice as tall and fluffy | They would not rise at all | They would turn bright blue
test: In a cooking game, a kid stirs hot caramel with his finger to taste it. What's wrong? | Hot caramel is much hotter than boiling water and sticks to skin | Nothing is wrong, because golden caramel is only ever just warm to touch | Caramel is cold when it is golden | It is fine if you lick it fast
test: "why does cutting onions make me cry but the whole onion in the bag doesn't" Best answer? | The stinging chemicals are made only when the onion is cut | Whole onions are always kept frozen in the shop, so they cannot sting | Your eyes only see cut onions | Bags soak up all the smell
test: Why do microwave instructions say "stir halfway"? | So the hot and cooler parts mix and heat evenly | So the microwave has a chance to cool down | So the soup does not go bad | So the bowl stays clean
test: Your friend's popcorn is old and half the kernels won't pop. Why? | They have dried out and have too little water inside | They are too fresh and still soft | They have too much water in them | Old kernels are made of stone
test: Why does fried chicken taste different from boiled chicken? | Frying is hot enough for browning reactions, boiling is not | Boiling adds salt to the chicken and frying never adds any at all | Fried chicken is a different animal | Boiled chicken has no protein
test: "takeaway rice been on the side all night, just nuke it" What is the risk? | Germs may have grown in it, which can upset your tummy | None, a microwave always kills every germ and every poison | It will explode in the microwave | It will turn back into hard rice
test: What would happen if you boiled an egg for 25 minutes? | The yolk would be hard and dry, maybe with a green ring | The yolk would melt and go runny again | The egg would turn into a chick | The shell would turn into jelly
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the browning of meat in a hot pan? | A reaction between sugars and proteins producing new flavour molecules | The deposition of pigment from the pan upon the surface of the meat | The rusting of iron contained in the meat | A purely physical change wholly reversible on cooling
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a popcorn kernel bursts upon heating. | The water within is converted to steam, raising the pressure within the shell | Air from without is forced into the kernel by the heat of the pan until it ruptures | The oil of the pan ignites within the kernel | The kernel contracts upon heating
exam: It is given that fresh pineapple has been added to a jelly mixture. Ascertain the probable consequence. | The jelly fails to set, the gelatine being broken down | The jelly sets more firmly and more rapidly than usual | The jelly turns to ice | The jelly acquires a sweeter flavour alone
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the storage of cooked rice. | It should be cooled promptly and refrigerated, certain germs multiplying rapidly at room temperature | It may be kept at room temperature indefinitely | It should be stored in a warm place to preserve its softness, refrigeration rendering it hard and unfit for consumption | It becomes poisonous upon refrigeration
